ance tasks to run  scientific experiments and so on  you know in the disability community we  use this phrase called universal design  and i think what the para astronaut  program is pushing us to think about is  again designing our environments so that  they're more universal and suited for  everybody and every type of ability  level you know from day one without  having to retrofit it later down the  road so i think that thinking about  universal design as it relates to to  settlements in other planets or space in  general helps us to really reframe that  and to think about you know whether  you're an infant or someone who's 100  years old whether you're large or small  whether you have a visible disability or  not you know what what type of  environment can we envision that reduces  the barriers for everyone to the  greatest extent possible  some of the adaptive support systems  these individuals use on a daily basis  may no longer be needed in space  an environment that could level the  playing field for many who are  considered disabled on earth  when you look at the international space  station  everybody floats up there your use of  your feet  in space isn't really needed and in fact  astronauts come back with very soft  feet because they haven't been used  for all that time so um the adaptations  on the space station they will be  considered but i imagine them to be  not massively  challenging because of the environment  that you are in  once you're in zero-g environment the  legs are not tremendously useful of  course the astronauts spend a lot of  time doing physical exercise to maintain  their health so that when they come back  to earth they're in a good state because  if you don't do so you lose muscle mass  and you also lose bone mass  astronauts can lose as much as 10  percent of bone density during six  months in space  to offset the effect they exercise for  two hours a day at least six days a week  they do an hour of cardio perhaps  running on a treadmill and exercising on  a bicycle and they do an hour of what  what you and i might refer to as  weightlifting now in space you can't  lift weights nothing has a weight  because you don't feel gravity um but we  use um sort of hydraulic springs like  like resistance uh to tension those  those muscles and so on  maintaining bone and muscle mass will be  critical for para astronauts  but as humans venture further from earth  more room on rockets and space stations  is needed for fuel so space is at a  premium  [Music]  one innovation that could let exercise  take up less space and time is the high  frequency impulse for microgravity or  hi-fi m  hi-fi m promises to reduce the  astronauts current workouts to just six  minutes a day on one machine  it does this by enabling astronauts to  jump in zero gravity  [Music]  so to be able to jump is a fantastic way  to lower the skeletal system build up  muscle density and also cardiovascularly  it's ferocious on the body  through esa bed rest studies we've  proven that jumping will mitigate  bone density and muscle mass loss with  just  50 jumps a day that's four to six  minutes a day that's amazing the time  that's going to give astronauts back to  do experiments and more science in space  but you can't just jump because you've  got to make sure the load and vibration  does not go through the spacecraft so  hi-fi m is designed to absorb its own  load and vibration when an individual is  jumping  hi-fi m has been developed with both  astronauts and para astronauts in mind  former royal marine lee spencer has been  trialling the machine throughout its  prototype phase  my left leg was really badly damaged  when i lost  my right leg and that really means that  i can't actually sprint or jump but the  great thing that this does  is it allows you as an amputee to train  in a way that i've not been able to  train before and that's with explosive  muscle movements where you you can jump  again  we can actually control the load t