ture missions including a trip to the  international space station by 2025  but beyond making space more accessible  the esa is also hoping a diverse crew  will give them an even greater chance of  success in future missions  [Music]  we know that diverse teams are better  teams we know that people that don't all  have the same life experience and don't  have all think in the same way come from  different countries work better together  it's the fantastic thing about working  the european space agency we're 22  different countries working together  and those different cultural backgrounds  and ways of thinking make us uh strong  and the same is true if you've got a  crew trying to go to mars the first time  you want a mix of people  different genders different backgrounds  different countries to make a stronger  team  the space industry is known for its  innovation  and the research and findings from the  feasibility program might not only  produce the first para astronaut but  also have an impact on how disability  and adaptation are treated back here on  earth  a lot of the adaptive technology that we  use here in the us  you know the speed of research and  development the speed of innovation  sometimes can be a little bit stagnant  and so i think that reframing that and  thinking about well how do you  best empower people with disabilities in  a space environment  how do you best empower people to be  astronauts has the ability to translate  to really push us a lot more quickly in  our innovation here  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

